{\b Chapter 3 Theoretical Framework, Conceptual Framework, and Hypotheses}\par
\par
{\b 3.1 Introduction}\par
This chapter presents the theoretical framework, conceptual framework, hypotheses, and operational definitions of the variables used in this study. Chapter 2 reviewed the literature on micro-credentials, employability, legitimacy, trust, and perceived value. Building on that foundation, the present chapter explains how the selected theories are applied to the current study and how they support the proposed relationships among recognition, stackability, industry endorsement, student trust, and perceived value. The chapter also defines the study variables in operational terms and presents the hypotheses to be tested.\par
\par
{\b 3.2 Theoretical Framework}\par
The present study is guided by an integrated theoretical framework built around signaling theory, legitimacy and trust logic, and perceived value in higher education. These perspectives are used together because the study focuses on how students evaluate the meaning of a micro-credential before they have direct evidence of its actual long-term payoff. In such circumstances, visible credential features are likely to shape how students judge whether the credential is credible and worthwhile.\par
\par
{\b 3.2.1 Signaling Theory in This Study}\par
Signaling theory is the primary theoretical foundation of this study. Spence (1973) explains that under conditions of uncertainty and information asymmetry, actors use observable signals to infer less visible qualities. In the present context, students cannot directly know whether an IT micro-credential will carry real academic or labor-market value before they pursue it. They therefore rely on visible features of the credential itself.\par
\par
Recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement are treated in this study as visible signals. Formal recognition suggests institutional standing. Stackability suggests that the credential can contribute to a larger educational pathway and therefore has future utility. Industry endorsement suggests practical relevance and external validation. From a signaling perspective, these features reduce uncertainty and help students make judgments about the likely worth of a micro-credential.\par
\par
{\b 3.2.2 Legitimacy and Trust in This Study}\par
Signaling theory explains why visible cues matter, but legitimacy and trust logic explain why those cues may lead to acceptance and confidence. Deephouse et al. (2017) argue that legitimacy is based on perceived appropriateness within a broader system of norms, values, and rules. Applied to micro-credentials, this means that a credential is more likely to be trusted when it appears to fit accepted educational and institutional structures.\par
\par
In the present study, student trust is conceptualized as the student-level expression of these legitimacy judgments. Students assess whether a credential appears credible, reliable, and institutionally meaningful. Ohanian (1990) further strengthens this logic by showing that judgments of trustworthiness and expertise can be operationalized in a credible measurement framework. Although Ohanian's work was developed in a different context, its credibility logic is relevant here because students may judge the trustworthiness of a credential in a similar cue-based way. Accordingly, this study proposes that recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement shape perceived value partly through their influence on student trust.\par
\par
{\b 3.2.3 Perceived Value Logic in This Study}\par
Perceived value is treated as the main outcome variable of the study because it captures more than simple interest, curiosity, or behavioral intention. Alves (2010) argues that perceived value in higher education can be understood as a trade-off involving benefits, price-quality judgment, and comparison with alternatives. Amado-Mateus et al. (2023) further show that perceived value can be measured in a structured and psychometrically meaningful way within higher education settings.\par
\par
This study adopts perceived value as the primary dependent variable because it reflects whether students judge a micro-credential to be genuinely worthwhile relative to the effort required. A student may express interest in a credential for many reasons, but perceived value goes further by asking whether the credential appears to offer meaningful academic or career benefit. From the perspective of the present study, visible signals influence trust, and trust in turn shapes this broader judgment of value.\par
\par
{\b 3.3 Conceptual Framework}\par
Based on the theoretical framework discussed above, the study proposes that formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement influence student trust and perceived value of IT micro-credentials. Student trust is expected to function as a mediating variable between the visible credential signals and perceived value. In addition, perceived value may be linked to enrollment intention as an exploratory outcome, but enrollment intention is not part of the core hypothesis set.\par
\par
