The conceptual relationships may be summarized as follows:\par
1. Formal recognition influences student trust.\par
2. Stackability influences student trust.\par
3. Industry endorsement influences student trust.\par
4. Student trust influences perceived value.\par
5. Formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement may also directly influence perceived value.\par
6. Student trust mediates the effect of the three signal variables on perceived value.\par
\par
{\b 3.4 Hypotheses Development}\par
\par
{\b 3.4.1 Recognition and Student Trust}\par
Recognition is expected to increase student trust because it signals that a micro-credential has formal standing within an accepted educational system. OECD (2021) stresses that recognition and transparency are central to the quality and value of micro-credentials. Varadarajan et al. (2023) also show that institutions and employers value clarity, trust, and integration into recognized systems. When a credential is formally acknowledged by universities or related credit structures, students are more likely to see it as legitimate and dependable. Therefore, the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H1: Formal recognition positively affects student trust in IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.2 Stackability and Student Trust}\par
Stackability is expected to increase student trust because it indicates that the credential is connected to a broader educational pathway rather than functioning as an isolated certificate. Ahsan et al. (2023) emphasize the modular nature of micro-credentials, while OECD (2023) highlights the importance of pathways and flexibility in lifelong learning. If students perceive that a credential can contribute to future academic or professional progression, they may be more likely to trust its usefulness and legitimacy. Thus, the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H2: Stackability positively affects student trust in IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.3 Industry Endorsement and Student Trust}\par
Industry endorsement is expected to increase student trust because it signals relevance beyond the university environment. Ha et al. (2024) show that employer understanding and perceptions matter for the effectiveness of micro-credentials, while Sharma et al. (2024) and Zou et al. (2024) reinforce the importance of employability-related outcomes. When a credential is visibly associated with employers or professional bodies, students may interpret this as evidence that the credential has real-world significance. Therefore, the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H3: Industry endorsement positively affects student trust in IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.4 Student Trust and Perceived Value}\par
Student trust is expected to positively influence perceived value because students are more likely to regard a credential as worthwhile when they believe it is credible and dependable. Legitimacy logic suggests that trust grows when a credential appears appropriate and meaningful within recognized structures (Deephouse et al., 2017). Perceived value literature also indicates that value judgments depend on whether the offering appears to justify the required sacrifice (Alves, 2010; Amado-Mateus et al., 2023). If students do not trust the credential, they are unlikely to assign high value to it. Therefore, the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H4: Student trust positively affects perceived value of IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.5 Recognition and Perceived Value}\par
Recognition may also directly influence perceived value because it reduces uncertainty and makes the credential appear more meaningful within formal education. A recognized credential may be seen as offering stronger academic benefit, clearer transferability, and better future use. In line with the broader literature on recognition and value (OECD, 2021; Varadarajan et al., 2023), the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H5: Formal recognition positively affects perceived value of IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.6 Stackability and Perceived Value}\par
Stackability may directly influence perceived value because it implies that effort spent on the credential can contribute to larger future outcomes. A credential that can be accumulated into further qualifications may appear more worthwhile than one that remains standalone. This aligns with the pathway logic found in OECD (2023) and the modular qualification logic described by Ahsan et al. (2023). Therefore, the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H6: Stackability positively affects perceived value of IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.7 Industry Endorsement and Perceived Value}\par
Industry endorsement may directly influence perceived value because students may interpret endorsement as evidence of practical relevance and stronger employability value. Prior studies suggest that micro-credentials are often evaluated in terms of career outcomes, relevance, and external recognition (Sharma et al., 2024; Zou et al., 2024). A credential that appears aligned with industry expectations may therefore be judged as more valuable. Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed:\par
\par
H7: Industry endorsement positively affects perceived value of IT micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 3.4.8 Mediating Role of Student Trust}\par
The integrated framework of this study suggests that student trust functions as an important mechanism through which the three visible signal variables influence perceived value. Recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement are expected to shape whether students view the credential as legitimate and believable. Once trust is formed, students are more likely to assign higher value to the credential. In this way, trust links external signals to the final judgment of worth. Therefore, the following mediation hypotheses are proposed:\par
\par
H8: Student trust mediates the relationship between formal recognition and perceived value.\par
H9: Student trust mediates the relationship between stackability and perceived value.\par
H10: Student trust mediates the relationship between industry endorsement and perceived value.\par
\par
{\b 3.5 Operational Definitions of Variables}\par
\par
{\b Formal Recognition}\par
Formal recognition refers to the extent to which a micro-credential is officially acknowledged by universities, academic systems, or recognized learning-credit structures. In this study, formal recognition is operationalized as an experimental vignette manipulation indicating whether the hypothetical IT micro-credential is formally recognized by participating Thai universities and recordable within a recognized credit structure.\par
\par
{\b Stackability}\par
Stackability refers to the extent to which a micro-credential can be accumulated, combined, or transferred toward a larger qualification or academic credit pathway. In this study, stackability is operationalized as an experimental vignette manipulation indicating whether the hypothetical IT micro-credential can count toward a future certificate or academic pathway.\par
\par
{\b Industry Endorsement}\par
Industry endorsement refers to the extent to which a micro-credential is supported, validated, reviewed, or associated with recognized employers, professional bodies, or industry-related organizations. In this study, industry endorsement is operationalized as an experimental vignette manipulation indicating whether the hypothetical IT micro-credential is endorsed by a recognized IT industry body or employer-related group.\par
\par
{\b Student Trust}\par
Student trust refers to students' belief that an IT micro-credential is credible, dependable, legitimate, and worthy of confidence. In this study, student trust is measured through a five-item Likert-type scale adapted to the micro-credential context and informed by credibility and legitimacy logic.\par
\par
{\b Perceived Value}\par
Perceived value refers to students' overall judgment that an IT micro-credential offers meaningful academic, professional, or personal benefit relative to the effort and sacrifice required to obtain it. In this study, perceived value is measured through a Likert-type scale adapted from higher education perceived-value logic and refined for the micro-credential context.\par
\par
{\b Enrollment Intention}\par
Enrollment intention refers to students' stated willingness to pursue or consider pursuing a similar IT micro-credential in the future. In this study, enrollment intention is treated as an exploratory outcome rather than a core dependent variable.\par
\par
{\b 3.6 Chapter Summary}\par
This chapter presented the theoretical framework, conceptual framework, hypotheses, and operational definitions of the study variables. Signaling theory explains why students may rely on visible credential features when evaluating micro-credentials under uncertainty. Legitimacy and trust logic explain why those features may influence whether the credential appears credible and worthy of confidence. Perceived value is treated as the primary outcome because it captures a broader judgment of worth than simple interest or intention. On the basis of these theories, the study proposes that formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement influence student trust and perceived value, with trust acting as a mediator. The next chapter describes the research methodology used to test these hypotheses.\par
